# Audit checkpoints — operational evidence, not source.
#
# The default location is relative to the working directory, so on a hub started
# from a clone they land inside the repository. That matters because the
# development workflow here uses `git add -A` on every patch: without this rule,
# a deployment's audit evidence eventually gets committed to a public repo by
# accident. They are also per-day files that would accumulate forever.
#
# Point DOSYNC_CHECKPOINT_DIR somewhere outside the clone (e.g.
# /var/lib/dosync/checkpoints) to keep evidence and source properly apart.
checkpoints/

# Deployment state and backups — never in the repository.
#
# Two empty .tgz files reached main on 2026-08-08 because a `tar` that had
# failed left them behind and `git add -A` picked them up. Empty, so nothing
# leaked — but the same command would have committed dosync.db, which on the
# reference deployment is a 42,000-entry audit chain, and `*.db` was not covered
# either. The only thing that had prevented it was that nobody ran `git add -A`
# from the Pi.
#
# Since 0.4.3 state lives outside the source tree by default (see
# docs/DEPLOYMENT-LAYOUT.md). These patterns cover deployments that predate it,
# which includes the reference hub.
*.tgz
*.tar.gz
*.tar
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
audit-segments/

# The RAW production registry carries real room names; only the anonymized
# snapshot (prod_registry_anonymized.json) belongs in the repository.
benchmarks/fixtures/prod_registry.json
